<section class="bg-base-100 text-base-content">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[80rem] px-5 pt-12 pb-20 md:px-8 md:pt-16 md:pb-24">
    <p class="label text-base-content/75">Visit · 58 Gilder Street</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-[18ch] text-[clamp(2.75rem,5vw+1rem,5.25rem)] leading-[1.02]">A pharmacy, then a bookshop</h1>
    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-14">
      <img src="/media/corner-building.jpg" class="aspect-[16/10] w-full rounded-box object-cover object-[50%_18%] lg:col-span-7">
      <div class="body-copy space-y-5 text-[1.125rem] lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p>For seventy-five years the corner of Gilder and Dexter was Almeida's Pharmacy. The oak apothecary drawers that held the powders now hold the poetry and the postcards, the pressed-tin ceiling is still there, and so is the marble soda counter, six stools long.</p>
        <p>Nadia Fontes spent eleven years driving to other people's bookshops as a publisher's sales rep, from Bar Harbor to New Haven. In 2019 she opened her own, a block from the Armory, with 12,000 books and a shelf of signed copies.</p>
      </div>
